{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>All the souls of the house of Jacob who came to Egypt: seventy.</b> They were seventy besides Jacob, for so it is written: ״ויהי כל נפש יוצאי ירך יעקב שבעים נפש״, and with him 71. And thus (Numbers 11:16): ״אספה לי שבעים איש מזקני ישראל״, and with Moses 71. And likewise the Great Sanhedrin in the Temple was seventy, and with the Nasi who was over them 71. And likewise the ancient nations were 70, and with Israel 71. And all this is a model of above, corresponding to the seventy angels that surround the Throne of Glory, and with YHWH the Elohim of Israel 71, and they are called His court. And therefore, when it mentioned the seventy, it did not say 'to Jacob' as it mentioned in the verse above it, but it said 'to the house of Jacob,' for Jacob together with his house alludes to the court of the Holy One, blessed be He, and this is the 71." "<b>And reflect</b> from this on the greatness of Jacob, that seventy souls emanated from his potency, from whom emerged sixty myriad, corresponding to the seventy names that emanated from the Shekhinah, whose number is no less than sixty myriad, and they are the culmination of all the supernal notions; therefore Israel were not worthy to receive the Torah until they reached sixty myriad, for then they corresponded to the supernal number, and then the Torah was given with the agreement of all. And the Shekhinah does not rest upon Israel except in a number of sixty myriad, which is six hundred thousand. And the matter is that the Shekhinah is comprised of six extremities, and in six hundred thousand there are six times one hundred thousand, which are ten myriad, for each and every extremity ten myriad. And from the day the Shekhinah rested upon Mount Sinai at the giving of the Torah it did not depart from Israel until the First Temple was destroyed, and from the day the First Temple was destroyed the Shekhinah did not rest upon Israel, for in the Second Temple the Shekhinah did not rest. And the intention is not to say that the Shekhinah did not rest in the Second Temple at all, for we find that the Shekhinah rests upon two myriad, from what is written (Numbers 10:36): ״שובה ה׳ רבבות אלפי ישראל״, and it is written (Psalms 68:18): ״רכב אלהים רבותים אלפי שנאן ה׳ בם״. All the more so upon four myriad does the Shekhinah rest, and furthermore the verse testifies (Haggai 1:8): ״וארצה בו ואכבד״ — that is, I will cause the Glory to dwell in it, which is the Shekhinah. But it did not rest there continually as in the First Temple, because there were not sixty myriad there, for they went up from Babylon only forty thousand, as it is written (Ezra 2:64): ״כל הקהל כאחד ארבע רבוא״. And this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded on (Haggai 1:8): ״וארצה בו ואכבדה״ — ואכבד is written, missing the letter ה, because five things were missing from there: the Ark, the anointing oil, fire, the Shekhinah, and the Urim and Thummim. And because of this it says ואכבד, to explain that the Glory was there — which is the Shekhinah, not the Tiferet; and this is ואכבד to diminish the Tiferet. And the missing ה, which is the last letter of the Name, is on account that the Shekhinah was missing from there, since it is one of the five things that were missing. And how could it be missing, when the verse says ואכבדה? Rather, it did not rest there continually as in the First Temple but only hovered. But in the time to come Israel will be numerous without number, as it is written (Hosea 2:1): ״והיה מספר בני ישראל כחול הים״. It did not say ״והיה זרע ישראל כחול הים״ but ״והיה מספר״ — that is, those who were counted in the number, from twenty years old and upward, will be like the sand of the sea, and then the Tiferet and the Glory will return; and thus Isaiah promised (Isaiah 60:1): ״קומי אורי כי בא אורך וכבוד ה׳ עליך זרח״. And understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {1 "The word התבונן ('and reflect') opens the comment in the Hebrew without a separate method-header; rendered as a continuation of the verse-lemma. The technical Kabbalistic term תכלית כל הדעות ('culmination of all the notions') is rendered literally without decoding its sefirotic allusion. The Ezra population figure (40,000) is cited by Rabbeinu Bahya as ארבע רבוא and copied as such; the canonical total in Ezra 2:64 is 42,360."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:59.053962390Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}